WEBVTT

00:00:00.506 --> 00:00:06.546 A:middle
[ Sound Effects ]

00:00:07.046 --> 00:00:09.886 A:middle
Hello. I'm Fran Chiaramonte,
Program Scientist

00:00:09.886 --> 00:00:14.636 A:middle
for Physical Sciences aboard
the International Space Station.

00:00:15.156 --> 00:00:16.926 A:middle
And today I'm going
to talk to you

00:00:16.926 --> 00:00:18.716 A:middle
about the Light Microscopy
Module.

00:00:19.136 --> 00:00:21.366 A:middle
First of all, I'd like
to mention the fact

00:00:21.366 --> 00:00:25.116 A:middle
that we've received two awards
from the ISS research program.

00:00:25.586 --> 00:00:28.286 A:middle
One for the CBB experiment,

00:00:28.906 --> 00:00:31.036 A:middle
which is a heat pipe-related
research.

00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:34.436 A:middle
And the second for
plant research

00:00:34.516 --> 00:00:36.946 A:middle
in which they use
biomarkers to find

00:00:36.946 --> 00:00:39.336 A:middle
out what genes are expressed
when you go into microgravity.

00:00:39.336 --> 00:00:41.776 A:middle
And we are very proud
of that work

00:00:41.956 --> 00:00:43.266 A:middle
that our researchers have done.

00:00:43.816 --> 00:00:47.646 A:middle
In 2014, Proctor and
Gamble received an award

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:50.356 A:middle
from the ISS program as well.

00:00:50.796 --> 00:00:55.646 A:middle
And their work was looking at
colloidal systems and the effect

00:00:55.646 --> 00:00:58.586 A:middle
of colloids on commercial
product stabilizers.

00:00:59.006 --> 00:01:00.726 A:middle
Now, for those of you
that are interested

00:01:00.726 --> 00:01:03.206 A:middle
in conducting research
using the LMM,

00:01:03.886 --> 00:01:05.646 A:middle
in addition to many
different areas,

00:01:05.646 --> 00:01:08.886 A:middle
we are soliciting
proposals in biofilms

00:01:09.346 --> 00:01:14.706 A:middle
in which the LMM will be the
key device in which to examine

00:01:14.706 --> 00:01:17.406 A:middle
and do the research
for this areas.

00:01:17.916 --> 00:01:19.406 A:middle
Biofilms are very important

00:01:19.406 --> 00:01:21.646 A:middle
to NASA-this is something
that is undesired.

00:01:22.046 --> 00:01:24.496 A:middle
We are looking for
materials that are resistant

00:01:24.496 --> 00:01:28.306 A:middle
to biofilms aboard the
International Space Station.

00:01:28.366 --> 00:01:29.706 A:middle
We can put it to use right away.

00:01:30.166 --> 00:01:33.666 A:middle
And ultimately for
crude missions to Mars

00:01:33.986 --> 00:01:37.916 A:middle
where the astronauts have to
remain a long period of time

00:01:38.216 --> 00:01:40.706 A:middle
in a very small,
enclosed capsule,

00:01:41.096 --> 00:01:45.336 A:middle
and the bacteria can grow, and
this all must be contained.

00:01:45.836 --> 00:01:48.036 A:middle
And we feel that this
is an important area

00:01:48.406 --> 00:01:50.946 A:middle
to help not only people
on Earth but also NASA

00:01:50.946 --> 00:01:52.546 A:middle
in its long duration missions.

00:01:53.296 --> 00:01:58.556 A:middle
So, currently in the program we
have two new modules being built

00:01:58.786 --> 00:02:00.046 A:middle
that will use the LMM.

00:02:00.046 --> 00:02:03.406 A:middle
And this is a temperature
gradient module

00:02:03.406 --> 00:02:05.446 A:middle
and an electric field module.

00:02:05.776 --> 00:02:09.676 A:middle
Both of these allow us to
begin the process of being able

00:02:09.676 --> 00:02:11.636 A:middle
to manipulate the particles

00:02:12.076 --> 00:02:15.246 A:middle
that our colloidal
scientist will be studying.

00:02:15.556 --> 00:02:18.466 A:middle
As opposed to a randomly
ordered system,

00:02:18.466 --> 00:02:21.896 A:middle
we can now control the
orientation of the particles

00:02:21.896 --> 00:02:24.316 A:middle
and begin to look at changing
the material properties

00:02:24.586 --> 00:02:26.056 A:middle
of these soft matter physics.

00:02:27.786 --> 00:02:31.026 A:middle
So, to ramp up where we
are and what we've done,

00:02:32.046 --> 00:02:34.676 A:middle
the LMM was launched in 2009.

00:02:35.336 --> 00:02:40.546 A:middle
Since then, we have successfully
completely 12 experiments.

00:02:41.066 --> 00:02:47.196 A:middle
And we plan to run
15 to 20 more.

00:02:47.196 --> 00:02:50.146 A:middle
So, as far as I can see,

00:02:50.736 --> 00:02:53.216 A:middle
the program looks very
strong, very robust.

00:02:53.866 --> 00:02:57.176 A:middle
And the way I like to look at
it, we're holding all aces.

00:02:57.176 --> 00:02:57.966 A:middle
Thank you for your time.

00:02:58.516 --> 00:03:05.650 A:middle
[ Silence ]

